  <h1>Cassette vs Dipstick (Strip) Test</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> A cassette encloses the test strip in a plastic housing for hygienic, labeled sample handling; a dipstick/strip is a bare test strip that is dipped into the sample. Both use the same lateral-flow chemistry — the difference is format and cost.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Cassette</th><th>Dipstick / Strip</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Format</th><td>Enclosed plastic housing</td><td>Bare strip</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Sample handling</th><td>Drop into well</td><td>Dip into sample</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hygiene / safety</th><td>Higher (enclosed)</td><td>Lower (open strip)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Labeling</th><td>Printable surface</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Typical use</th><td>Clinical / professional</td><td>High-volume, bulk, retail</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each format is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cassette:</strong> the strip is sealed inside plastic with a sample well and result window — better for infectious samples and professional labeling.</li>
    <li><strong>Dipstick:</strong> the strip is exposed and dipped directly into a specimen container — cheapest and fastest for bulk use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cassette:</strong> containment, clear labeling, professional presentation.</li>
    <li><strong>Dipstick:</strong> lowest cost, minimal packaging, high-volume throughput.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cassette:</strong> higher cost and packaging weight.</li>
    <li><strong>Dipstick:</strong> open sample exposure, no labeling surface, easier to mishandle.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Specimen considerations</h2>
  <p>For urine and liquid specimens, dipsticks are common; for blood, swab or potentially infectious samples, cassettes are preferred for containment.</p>

  <h2>Procurement considerations</h2>
  <p>Clinics typically buy cassettes; retail and high-volume screening programs favor strips for cost. Both can be private-labeled.</p>

